Across 52 Asian coral snake bites in Thailand: no ptosis, bulbar weakness, respiratory compromise, paralysis or death; no ventilation or antivenom. 
The authors state CK was not systematically measured and subclinical myotoxicity could not be ruled out — so the largest series available is silent on the question §37.5.6 answers.
The dose question is settled: Takasaki’s was a wet bite from a snake that can yield ~150 mg in one milking.

3. Mechanism A: Direct Myotoxicity
3.1 What the venom contains
Tan et al. 2016 (C. bivirgata flaviceps): 23 proteins across 11 families, predominated by PLA2 and cytotoxin, no detectable post-synaptic neurotoxin. 
Moderate cytotoxicity against fibroblasts and myotoxicity in mice. ~41% PLA2, ~23% cytotoxin-like.
Tan et al. 2019 (C. intestinalis, Ismail a co-author): PLA2 43.4%, 3FTx 20.1%. In vivo myotoxic but non-lethal to mice, frogs and geckos even at 5–10 µg/g.

3.2 The “cytotoxin” problem
The word cytotoxin does a great deal of work in this literature, and it is not earned.
The annotations are by homology. No purified Calliophis “cytotoxin” has been shown to lyse cells.

Yang et al. made this criticism in 2016. Earlier studies concluding the venom’s 3FTx were exclusively cytotoxic reflected a very narrow scope of assays, 
with cytotoxicity assumed from partial-sequence similarity rather than established by activity characterisation. Their own work then found a 3FTx doing something else entirely.
Dashevsky and Fry went further in 2021. Although cytotoxins are close neighbours of the clades containing the δ-elapitoxins and maticotoxins, the 
studies describing both noted that they are not themselves cytotoxic. The containing clade sits in a polytomy that also includes a large group of 
Micrurus toxins never shown to be cytotoxic. And the loop closes. Tasoulis et al. state that maticotoxin alone makes up 23% of the whole venom — 
identifying the “cytotoxin-like” fraction as the toxin Takasaki characterised and found not to be cytotoxic.
Even the authors who use the label hedge it. Palasuberniam et al. propose these as Y-type CTX-like proteins, preserving a less toxic form of elapid CTX-like protein.

3.3 What direct evidence of cytotoxicity actually exists
One assay. Tan et al. 2016 measured crude venom against CRL-2648 fibroblasts: IC50 62.14 ± 0.87 µg/mL, described as moderate. Two limitations:
1. It is crude venom — it cannot attribute the effect to any component. PLA2 at 41% would produce membrane damage unaided.
2. The figure is high for a venom alleged to be cytotoxin-rich. Genuinely cytotoxin-dominated elapid venoms are considerably more potent in this kind of assay.
Nothing else exists. The nanofractionation platforms developed since 2021 profile elapid cytotoxicity across human cell lines after separating components — 
precisely the method that would settle this. Applied to Naja, Bungarus and others. Calliophis has not been run.

3.4 Consequences
Mechanism A loses its cytotoxin component entirely. What remains is PLA2 at ~41%. The whole venom’s cytotoxicity and myotoxicity are real measured 
findings, but they were assigned to cytotoxin or cytotoxin–PLA2 synergy presumptively, on the same contested annotation. PLA2 alone accounts for both.
The abundance question partly resolves in favour of §5’s original claim. Calliotoxin is probably a genuinely minor protein component, and Mechanism 
B weakens accordingly.

What replaces the worry is a better question. A toxin at 23% of this venom, isolated thirty-five years ago, with no established function — 
not cytotoxic, not demonstrated at any channel.

3.5 The recommendation already exists
From Tan et al. 2019: serum creatine kinase should be monitored serially for potential complication of myotoxicity. This is the Handbook’s 
observation, in print, with a mechanism attached, from the same clinician.

3.6 How PLA2 myotoxicity works
Myotoxic PLA2s bind the sarcolemma and hydrolyse its phospholipids; the membrane loses integrity, calcium enters, cytosolic contents including CK 
escape. A direct coral-snake precedent: Micrurus nigrocinctus venom acts primarily on the sarcolemma, producing wedge-shaped “delta” lesions within 
15 minutes with rapid loss of muscle CK and creatine and calcium influx.

3.7 Why this explanation is still preferred
● PLA2 is dominant by mass, with an independently established myotoxic mechanism.
● Myotoxicity demonstrated in mice for both species.
● No assumptions about dose, kinetics or channel behaviour.
● Explains local, self-limiting CK elevation and the absence of neurotoxicity.
It remains parsimonious, though on a narrower base: PLA2 alone, rather than PLA2 plus a cytotoxin fraction that turns out not to be cytotoxic.

4. Mechanism B: NaV1.4 and Calliotoxin
4.1 The channel
NaV1.4 is the voltage-gated sodium channel of adult skeletal muscle, encoded by SCN4A (17q23.3) — the predominant isoform in adult skeletal muscle, 
generating the inward current behind the action-potential upstroke.The α-subunit folds into four homologous domains around a central pore, each with 
six transmembrane segments: S1–S4 form the voltage sensor (S4 carries positive charges that move outward on depolarisation); S5–S6 line the pore.
Three states, two gates: closed at ~−80 to −90 mV; open on depolarisation (sub-millisecond); then fast-inactivated, when the DIII–DIV linker swings 
across the inner mouth of the pore and plugs it. Fast inactivation is separate from closing the activation gate and can be disrupted independently. 
That is what calliotoxin does.

4.2 What calliotoxin does
