|-----------------------|--------------|--------------|---------------------|
|V½ activation          |−35.0 ± 0.1 mV|−38.1 ± 0.3 mV|Hyperpolarising shift|
|V½ fast inactivation   |−66.1 ± 0.5 mV|−61.6 ± 0.7 mV|Depolarising shift   |
|Inactivation τ (−20 mV)|0.24 ± 0.02 ms|5.22 ± 0.9 ms |~22× slowing         |
|Persistent current     |−6 ± 4 pA     |−536 ± 33 pA  |~90× increase        |
|Peak ramp current      |−143 ± 12 pA  |−728 ± 52 pA  |~5× increase         |
200 nM purified native toxin, HEK-293 cells expressing human NaV1.4. All effects abolished by tetrodotoxin; unaffected by nicotinic, muscarinic and 
α1 antagonists. In intact muscle: rising contractions, fasciculations, abolished nerve-mediated twitches. Yang et al. report no taxon-specific effect — 
so the mild clinical picture cannot be attributed to channel-level resistance.

4.3 The analogy to human SCN4A disease
Inference from here on.
Inherited gain-of-function SCN4A mutations cause sodium-channel myotonia, paramyotonia congenita, and hyperkalaemic/normokalaemic periodic paralysis. 
What impaired fast inactivation does to a living human:
● Myotonia — stiffness, delayed relaxation.
● Episodic weakness when depolarisation inactivates channels secondarily.
● Myotonic discharges on EMG.
● CK elevation. In 56 paramyotonia congenita patients (49 with SCN4A mutations), about half had mild weakness and most had increased serum CK. 
Values are modest: 848 U/L (T704M), 279–771 U/L (paediatric), 276 IU/L. Recurrent exertional rhabdomyolysis also reported in a paramyotonia family with SCN4A variants.
This establishes plausibility, not causation.

4.4 Precedents from other NaV activators
Scorpion envenoming. A documented rhabdomyolysis-and-myocarditis case: CK 2,129 U/L, CK-MB 43.24 ng/mL, troponin 22.59 ng/mL. Cardiac markers show this was 
not purely skeletal, and scorpion envenoming involves massive catecholamine release — the CK cannot be attributed cleanly to direct channel action.
Ciguatera. Ciguatoxins bind site 5 on voltage-sensitive channels in all excitable tissues; P-CTX-1 acts across multiple NaV isoforms. Symptoms include 
myalgia, arthralgia, paraesthesiae, dysaesthesiae and heightened nociception, some persisting months to years. The more interesting comparison for the Takasaki case.

5. On the “Low Abundance” Claim
Handbook §37.2.1.4 cites Yang et al. 2016. The citation appears misattributed — they did not quantify calliotoxin’s share, and their argument runs the other way.
Revision 2 doubted the claim; Revision 3 largely restores it. The abundant fraction is maticotoxin, and maticotoxins and δ-elapitoxins are distinct subclades — 
so it is not calliotoxin in disguise.
Conclusion: calliotoxin probably is a minor protein component, the Handbook’s claim is probably right, and only its citation is wrong. Mechanism B weakens correspondingly.
The transcript–protein divergence remains striking and unexplained — ~40% of toxin transcripts versus a minor share of secreted protein. Post-transcriptional 
regulation, differential secretion, or poor peptide recovery are all possible. None investigated.
Still worth doing: re-analysis of the existing proteomic spectra against the 2021 transcriptome, which did not exist when the proteome was annotated. It would 
put a number on calliotoxin directly rather than by subtraction. Computational reanalysis of published data, no new experiment.

