|Spatial distribution          |Confined to envenomed tissue         |Potentially wider                                        |
|Na+-channel blockade          |No effect                            |Mexiletine predicted to help                             |
|Histology                     |Delta lesions, sarcolemmal disruption|Fibre damage without primary lysis                       |
The single most informative test is needle EMG near the bite site in the acute phase. Myotonic discharges would be strong evidence that calliotoxin acts on 
human NaV1.4 in vivo — never sought, as far as I can determine. Their absence, with raised CK, would point firmly to Mechanism A.
Other predictions: serial CK with CK-MB; CK elevation commoner than clinical findings; myoglobinuria absent or trivial; species comparison (C. intestinalis vs 
C. bivirgatus — requires only existing poison-centre records); proteome reanalysis.
The maticotoxin gap
Separately from the CK question, the most conspicuous hole in this venom’s characterisation is a toxin making up roughly a quarter of it whose function 
is unknown. Maticotoxin was isolated in 1991, is not cytotoxic, and has not been tested at any ion channel or receptor. Only a partial sequence for maticotoxin 
A was published; gross amino acid compositions were given for the others, which Dashevsky and Fry note may never be matchable to specific sequences.

Three things worth doing, none requiring a patient:
1. Run it through a modern functional panel — nicotinic and muscarinic receptors, NaV subtypes, KV, CaV. Calliotoxin was found because somebody ran 
an assay nobody had thought to run; maticotoxin has had no equivalent attempt.
2. Test purified maticotoxin for cytotoxicity properly, by nanofractionation against human cell lines. Settles a thirty-five-year-old label either way.
3. Recover full sequences from the 2021 transcriptome and match them to the 1991 fractions.
If maticotoxin shares calliotoxin’s target, Mechanism B returns with a quarter of the venom behind it. If it is genuinely cytotoxic, Mechanism A is 
vindicated on evidence rather than assumption. Either result beats the present position.
Study design. Prospective cohort at a Malaysian or Thai centre: serial CK, CK-MB, renal function, potassium, urine myoglobin, needle EMG where feasible, 
stratified by species. Given seven confirmed C. bivirgatus bites in ten years in Thailand, this needs multi-centre collaboration. The retrospective comparison could be done now.

7. What Should Be Retired
“The majority of human C. bivirgatus envenomations were lethal.” Asserted on the basis of a single 1957 case with no surviving clinical description. 
Against it stand eight or more documented bites — including one large wet bite — with minor or local effects, no systemic neurotoxicity, no deaths.
That a bite is a medical emergency. The evidence supports assessment and short observation, which is what the Thai authors recommend as a precaution 
rather than on the basis of anything observed.

8. Caveats
● §37.5.6 is a personal communication: no case count, values, species breakdown, or treatment of confounders.
● CK is non-specific.
● Mechanism B remains unevidenced in humans.
● Calliotoxin’s subtype selectivity is unknown — only NaV1.4 was tested.
● The fatal case remains unexplained and probably always will.
● Species pooling and venom variation both limit interpretation.
● The cytotoxin critique has limits. §3.2 establishes that maticotoxin was characterised and found not cytotoxic, and that the label persisted anyway. 
It does not establish that maticotoxin has no membrane activity under conditions nobody has tested, nor that the minor 3FTx components are innocent. 
Crude venom cytotoxicity and myotoxicity are real measured findings; what is unresolved is which components produce them.
● The maticotoxin identification rests on one source. Tasoulis et al. state the 23% figure in a commentary on another paper, not a primary analysis of 
this venom. Consistent with the published proteome, but worth verifying against source data.

9. Conclusion
CK elevation in Calliophis bite patients is most likely direct myotoxicity, driven principally by the PLA2 fraction that dominates these venoms — an 
explanation already proposed in print, by the same clinician whose observation the Handbook records.
Whether calliotoxin also contributes through NaV1.4 is open and plausible on mechanism, but probably minor: the abundant three-finger toxin in this 
venom is maticotoxin, not calliotoxin. It remains worth excluding, because it is cheap to do and because a positive finding would be the first 
demonstration that this unusual toxin does anything at all in a person.
The larger prize is elsewhere. A toxin constituting roughly a quarter of this venom has no known function. Calliotoxin was found because somebody 
ran an assay nobody had thought to run. Maticotoxin has never had that attempt, and the label it carries is very likely the reason: a protein already 
filed under “cytotoxin” does not look like an open question.
Two broader lessons emerge, and they are versions of the same failure.
A reputation outran its evidence. The danger attributed to this snake rests on one undocumented death from 1957, amplified through citation into assertions 
about lethality the clinical record contradicts. An annotation outran its evidence. “Cytotoxin” entered this literature as a sequence-similarity label and 
has been read ever since as a demonstrated function — to the point of being offered as the explanation for clinical findings. Yang et al. flagged the problem 
in 2016; Dashevsky and Fry restated it in 2021. The label persists regardless, attached to a protein whose own 1991 characterisation contradicted it.
In both cases a provisional claim hardened through repetition into a fact nobody had established. Anyone working on this venom should treat the descriptive
vocabulary — “cytotoxin”, “medically important”, “potentially lethal” — as inherited assumptions rather than findings, and check what each one actually rests on.

